Rule 1 (start_dim should be less than or equal to end_dim, and both should be within the valid dimension range of the input tensor)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : int, v_3 : int} |= v_2 ≤ v_3 ∧ v_2 ≥ (0 - ndim(v_1)) ∧ v_2 < ndim(v_1) ∧ v_3 ≥ (0 - ndim(v_1)) ∧ v_3 < ndim(v_1)
>>
Rule 2 (If start_dim is equal to end_dim, the number of dimensions of input tensor should be greater than 0)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : int, v_3 : int} |= if v_2 = v_3 then ndim(v_1) > 0
>>
Rule 3 (If end_dim is -1, it means the last dimension, hence ndim of input must be larger than 0)
{v_1 : tensor, v_3 : int} |= if v_3 = -1 then ndim(v_1) > 0
>>
Rule 4 (If start_dim and end_dim are valid dimension indices, then start_dim should not come after end_dim when both converted to positive indices)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : int, v_3 : int} |= if v_2 ≥ (0 - ndim(v_1)) ∧ v_2 < ndim(v_1) ∧ v_3 ≥ (0 - ndim(v_1)) ∧ v_3 < ndim(v_1) then (if v_2 < 0 then v_2 + ndim(v_1) else v_2) ≤ (if v_3 < 0 then v_3 + ndim(v_1) else v_3)
>>
Rule 5 (if start_dim is zero, then for any end_dim, end_dim should be less than ndim of input)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : int, v_3 : int} |= if v_2 = 0 then v_3 < ndim(v_1)
>>
Rule 6 (If the number of dimensions is 0, then start_dim and end_dim should both be 0 or -1)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : int, v_3 : int} |= if ndim(v_1) = 0 then (v_2 = 0 ∨ v_2 = -1) ∧ (v_3 = 0 ∨ v_3 = -1)
